cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or these results, please cite the paper named in
  the title below through its DOI, together with the works listed under
  references that your use relies on.
title: >-
  Does Multi-Agent Confidence Predict Task Difficulty, or Only React to It?
  A Pre-registered Test on Perturb-seq
# GENERATED from paper/paper.tex's abstract by scripts/paper/render_publish_description.py -- do not edit by hand.
abstract: >-
  A multi-agent group-generation system, in which language-model agents
  propose, critique and vote over several rounds, spends test-time compute
  (computation at inference) uniformly across tasks as operated here. That a
  single agent's own vote distribution can gate its test-time compute is
  already reported by the CATTS method of Lee and colleagues (2026); we ask
  the multi-agent question: whether the distribution of stated confidences and
  critiques across a group of role-specialised agents predicts how hard a task
  is, which would let the group's compute be allocated adaptively. The testbed
  is a five-agent orchestrator that predicts gene-expression responses in
  Perturb-seq data (pooled CRISPR screens read out by single-cell RNA
  sequencing), inspired by the CellForge system and operated with the MassGen
  framework. We define four metrics on the record of a run — Agent Confidence
  Entropy, Critique Severity Dispersion, the change in the agents' mean stated
  confidence from the first round to the last, and Winner Flip Rate — and
  their composite, the Task Difficulty Index (TDI). We pre-register five
  hypotheses, each with a criterion: that a sweep of trained models attains
  low held-out error on each of two screens of the K562 cell line, Adamson
  2016 (CRISPR interference) and Norman 2019 (CRISPR activation); that the
  Architect, the agent that selects the prediction model (the backbone),
  varies its choice; that a TDI component ranks tasks by held-out error; and
  that a TDI calibrated on one screen transfers to the other. The design is 41
  held-out perturbations, called tasks (21 from Adamson, and 15 single-gene
  and 5 two-gene perturbations from Norman), a sweep of 27 trained-model
  records per task, and three runs of the agents per task, each of exactly
  three rounds. The agents are large language models from a pinned roster of
  Anthropic Claude models (Haiku 4.5 for the four roles that propose, Sonnet
  5.5 for the role that validates) whose served model is recorded and asserted
  per call. A run in which any agent step falls back to a rule-based default
  is invalid, and every run carries a provenance record. We find that four of
  the five pre-registered hypotheses are supported and the cross-dataset
  transfer hypothesis is not; every hypothesis could be evaluated. The sweep
  reaches the pre-registered error on both screens, and the Architect's choice
  of backbone varies above the pre-registered entropy floor. Within each
  screen, the drop in the agents' mean confidence across rounds, and the
  composite that includes it, rank tasks by held-out error, which meets the
  pre-registered ranking criterion. From the second round on, however, every
  agent's prompt carries the previous round's held-out error, so that drop is
  stated after an error has been shown. In an exploratory analysis, the mean
  confidence stated before any feedback does not rank tasks by error
  (Spearman's rank correlation ρ = 0.003 on Adamson and 0.214 on Norman, both
  confidence intervals including zero). Read with the design and that
  analysis, the pre-registered pass is consistent with stated confidence
  falling further where a larger error was shown; it is not evidence that the
  group's confidence anticipates difficulty. The composite fitted on Adamson
  did not meet the pre-registered transfer criterion on Norman (ρ = 0.286
  against a criterion of > 0.4). The point estimates for Agent Confidence
  Entropy, an evenness index over the agents, run against the pre-registered
  direction in both screens; this observation is exploratory and supports no
  claim. A single-round probe, and a rule that would recommend an agent
  configuration from it, are specified but not evaluated. The values reported
  in the earlier release of this work (v0.5.0) are superseded in full; the
  appendix lists the defects behind each.
